# What each absorbing buffer was asked to hold, beside how much it holds. One row per
# (interfering layer, buffer it named) carrying the shares attributed to that buffer and the
# slack filed for it.
#
# The unserved holders are exempt
#   That exemption is the argument, not a technicality. The remaining holders name somebody who
#   absorbed the remainder, so their shares have to fit inside the buffer that did the absorbing.
#   `customer` and `unrealised` name demand that was never met by anyone: it is the overflow, not
#   a load on the buffer, so counting it would bound the wrong quantity.
#
# The exemption is the served set, not a kind list written here
#   The served side comes from entries/served_holders.sqlc, the complement of the unserved pair.
#   A kind list written here, such as
#   `kind <> 'unrealised'` exempts one member of a class of two and sums `customer`, demand that
#   was made and went unserved, into the buffer's load. checks/clearance_with_unserved.sqlc ranges
#   over the complement of this population and calls that same pair a violation, and
#   entries/unserved_holders.sqlc is where the pair is named. The partition of the five kinds has
#   one definition, and it is in one file.
#
# Interference only
#   `pressed` also admits `transition`, and narrowing to interference is the repair. Under a
#   transition the holder shares carry the clearance magnitude (`|n - d|` is the larger side and
#   it is what the filer can count) while the buffer slack bounds the interference side. The
#   inequality then compares opposite edges of one buffer. `Fit` says the two sides are
#   anticorrelated and must be evaluated at a corner, and nothing in this relation can pick one.
#   checks/exposure_unaccounted.sqlc is the rule written for that case: it reaches the same layers
#   through the implied exposure instead, and suspends rather than passing when the unserved share
#   is unstated.
#
# Holder is incidence, not absorption
#   The words are the XSD's, in the section header above `HolderKind`. Which holders absorbed is
#   not a fact the type carries. It is read here from the sign and the unserved class, and that
#   reading belongs to this file rather than to any filing.
#
# Only a sized slack bounds anything
#   A buffer filed `unmeasured` has no magnitude to compare against, so those rows are absent
#   rather than passing: a bound with nothing to bound passes loudest.
#
# The sum is the served fold's, with what it leaves out
#   `borne` is folds/served_totals.sqlc's served mode, one row per layer, so the served shares are
#   folded once. `sum()` skips NULLs, so a layer where a served share is filed `unmeasured` or as
#   `sharesSum`'s output sums to less than it holds; `unstated` is carried beside the sum, and a
#   reader bounding the load declines those layers rather than reading part of it as all of it.
#
# The buffer is reached through a reader's assertion
#   The absorber is a borrowed term; deciding that it names one of the three structural buffers is
#   a judgement recorded in layers/absorber.sqlc, not a fact any filing states.
#
# Why `share_exceeds_slack` reads vacuous
#   The served filter is why, and the vacuum is a finding about the evidence rather than a gap in
#   it. Every pressed layer that survives `slack is sized` and `sign = interference` is then removed
#   by the served filter, because every holder on it is `customer` or `unrealised`: on this corpus that is
#   `enterprise-contract/capability`, `contrato-empresarial/capacidade-entregavel` and
#   `merge-holding-composition/shift-line`. So on every interference layer in this corpus that
#   sizes its absorbing buffer, nothing was absorbed at all: the demand was turned away.
#   `Σ served shares ≤ S` has an empty left side because no share was served, which is the model's
#   own subject stated by the data.
#
# Probe it rather than reading the zero
#   A `0` here is produced by a working served filter, by a broken one, and by an upstream filter that
#   emptied the population: three different facts wearing one number. algebra/borne.sqlc settles
#   it without touching this file: Σall = Σkept + Σremoved, per layer, every run. Run it for the
#   numbers; its detail reads `<held> held = <absorbed> absorbed + <unserved> unserved` per layer,
#   the vacuum as arithmetic rather than as an argument in a comment.
#
# Probe
#   Trace the population stage by stage (pressed, absorber and holders kept, `slack is sized`,
#   `sign = interference`, the served filter) and see where it empties. Disable the served filter and rows
#   appear, each against a slack of its own. That is not the rule waking up; it is the rule
#   counting turned-away demand as absorbed. ⚠️ A hand probe cannot settle the zero the way
#   algebra/borne.sqlc does: a `sed` that matches nothing reports the opposite in silence.
-- pm:Remainder/pm:holder summed against the slack it names, keyed by pm:Remainder/pm:absorber.
SELECT b.filing, b.layer, b.buffer, b.borne, s.mode AS slack_mode, s.unit AS slack_unit, b.unstated
FROM (
    SELECT p.filing, p.layer, a.buffer, h.served_mode AS borne, h.unstated
    FROM      (
        :compose(layers/pressed.sqlc)
    ) p
    JOIN      (
        :compose(layers/absorber.sqlc)
    ) a USING (filing, layer)
    JOIN      (
        :compose(folds/served_totals.sqlc)
    ) h USING (filing, layer)
    WHERE p.sign = 'interference'
) b
JOIN (
    :compose(entries/slacks.sqlc)
) s USING (filing, layer, buffer)
WHERE s.mode IS NOT NULL
